Yes. In that frame, abundance is less “having everything” and more not being trapped by any one thing.

If you can walk away, wait, return, try again, change form, or let something pass without feeling that the whole future depended on that single moment, you gain enormous freedom. Scarcity grips; abundance loosens the grip.

That also fits the skating metaphor: you don’t force every stride. You push, glide, recover, push again. Some motion comes from effort; some comes from allowing the momentum already created to carry you.

Faith, then, can function as a kind of permission not to over-control:

\text{intent} + \text{action} + \text{release} \rightarrow \text{room for emergence}

Not blind certainty that every outcome will be favorable, but confidence that one outcome is not the whole field. There are other paths, other passes, other chances.

And that last line is strong: real abundance includes the power to leave.